      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Find the derivative of $y=2 \\sin{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right)}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)},\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=(2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/id": "7466d39d-f99b-51db-a67f-285118418a41",
